    "updated_at": "2026-03-18T16:52:45Z",
    "body": "> The interaction between active WebSockets and bfcache is currently undefined in the HTML Standard\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#unloading-document-cleanup-steps says\n\n> For each [WebSocket](https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#websocket) object webSocket whose [relevant global object](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-relevant-global) is window, [make disappear](https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#make-disappear) webSocket.\n>\n> If this affected any [WebSocket](https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#websocket) objects, then [make document unsalvageable](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#make-document-unsalvageable) given document and \"[websocket](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#blocking-websocket)\".\n\nHowever, I'm not against changing this so that the page can be bfcached even though there were `WebSocket`s that were force-closed.\n\nPer https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#feedback-from-the-protocol:~:text=When%20the%20WebSocket%20connection%20is%20closed%2C%20possibly%20cleanly%2C%20the%20user%20agent%20must%20queue%20a%20task%20to%20run%20the%20following%20substeps%3A , when the websocket connection is closed, it will queue a task for the `error` and `close` events. Therefore the event or events will be fired after the page is restored from bfcache, and not during the unloading document cleanup steps. So that seems OK.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
