    "body": "> Physical network disconnections (like turning off Wi-Fi) actually do not fire onerror. They only fire onclose with code 1006. So we might not need to fire onerror here either?\n\nNetwork disconnection in Chrome does fire onerror (eventually).\n\n[RFC6455](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455#:~:text=If%20at%20any%20point%20the%20underlying%20transport%20layer%20connection%20is) says:\n\n >  If at any point the underlying transport layer connection is\n   unexpectedly lost, the client MUST _Fail the WebSocket Connection_.\n\n[The WebSockets Living Standard](https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#feedback-from-the-protocol:~:text=If%20the%20user%20agent%20was%20required%20to%20fail%20the%20WebSocket%20connection%2C%20or%20if%20the%20WebSocket%20connection%20was%20closed%20after%20being%20flagged%20as%20full%2C%20fire%20an%20event%20named%20error%20at%20the%20WebSocket%20object.%20%5BWSP%5D) says:\n> If the user agent was required to [fail the WebSocket connection](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455#section-7.1.7), or if [the WebSocket connection was closed](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455#section-7.1.4) after being flagged as full, [fire an event](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-event-fire) named error at the [WebSocket](https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#websocket) object. [[WSP]](https://websockets.spec.whatwg.org/#biblio-wsp)\n\nSo I think Chrome's behaviour is correct here.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
